# FS.GG.SDD — regenerable lifecycle output (ADR-0018).
# Per-work-item readiness views are generated by fsgg-sdd and regenerated by
# `fsgg-sdd refresh`. They are transient: ignore by role, never commit, and do
# not re-include per feature. See docs/reference/artifact-taxonomy.md.
readiness/*/

# Generic build & tooling output.
bin/
obj/
artifacts/
TestResults/
.tmp/
nuget-cache/

# Workspace-local restore/install output INSIDE the template source tree (FS.GG.Templates#386).
# The `templates/fs-gg-*/` directories are themselves working solutions, and each one's own README
# documents a regeneration command that must be run IN that directory. Two of those commands write
# a build-output directory that the list above does not name:
#   .nuget/        — FS.GG.Templates#380/#382/#384 set `<RestorePackagesPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).nuget/packages</RestorePackagesPath>`
#                    in all four templates' Directory.Build.props, so a workspace-private package
#                    folder is what makes a committed `contentHash` enforceable. The documented
#                    `dotnet restore <Workspace>.slnx --force-evaluate` therefore materializes it:
#                    measured at 118M (fable-game) and 75M (web).
#   node_modules/  — templates/fs-gg-web and templates/fs-gg-fable-game are npm workspaces with five
#                    package.json roots between them; `npm install` in any of them writes a
#                    Playwright/Vite/@babylonjs dev closure of hundreds of megabytes.
# Neither was ignored, so the documented maintenance action left an unignored tree that `git add -A`
# would commit. `*.nupkg` above hides only the archives inside such a folder, never the folder: the
# .nuspec/.sha512/lib payload beside them is what `git status` reported.
.nuget/
node_modules/

# Templates-local: packaging + editor cruft.
*.nupkg
*.user
.vs/
.idea/

# ADR-0067 §9 phase 4 (FS-GG/.github#1676). A GENERATED VIEW of .claude/skills, resolved at checkout
# by `scripts/skill-view generate` and never committed: a committed symlink checks out as a plain text
# file under `git -c core.symlinks=false` (the git-for-Windows default without Developer Mode) and BOTH
# agent runtimes then load zero skills and exit 0 saying nothing (ADR-0067 §6). The FS.GG.Kit
# materialize regenerates it and then asserts it is visible (FsggKitCheckSkillView, ADR-0067 §8).
/.agents/skills
